<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
     IchthysOS: declare android.software.voice_recognizers UNAVAILABLE on this device.

     Rationale
     The user does not want assistant or voice-search features. This one feature flag is the
     master switch for the whole voice-interaction subsystem:

       * VoiceInteractionManagerService.shouldEnableService() returns
         hasSystemFeature(FEATURE_VOICE_RECOGNIZERS) (unless
         config_forceVoiceInteractionServicePackage is set, which it is not here). With the
         feature gone, mEnableService is false and the service never binds ANY
         VoiceInteractionService - which is the only way an AlwaysOnHotwordDetector or a
         HotwordDetectionService can ever be created. That is the always-listening path.
       * Settings' DefaultVoiceInputPreferenceController gates the "Voice input" default-app
         picker on the same feature, so that entry disappears too.

     AOSP declares the feature in frameworks/native/data/etc/handheld_core_hardware.xml, which
     is a shared file we do not want to patch; SystemConfig supports <unavailable-feature> for
     exactly this case, and /product/etc/permissions is a partition where feature declarations
     are honoured (SystemConfig.productPermissionFlag includes ALLOW_FEATURES).

     What this does NOT do
     It does not touch android.speech.SpeechRecognizer / RecognitionService: an installed app
     that bundles its own recogniser (or a keyboard with its own voice typing) still works.
     Killing that would be collateral damage on ordinary apps, not an OS feature removal.
     It also does not remove the vendor SoundTrigger HAL, which is part of the Samsung/QCOM
     vendor image and is referenced by the vendor VINTF manifest - removing it there would risk
     a VINTF mismatch. It stays dormant: with no VoiceInteractionService there is nothing to
     enrol a keyphrase model against it.
-->
<permissions>
    <unavailable-feature name="android.software.voice_recognizers" />
</permissions>
